Upon running the final implementation of the models, I obtained some rather interesting results. Promoters scored consistently high across all k values, averaging 92.94% 
accuracy. Introns also performed rather well, with accuracy increasing as k increased and reaching a maximum of 87.36%. The accuracies for exons and repeats also rose 
alongside k, indicating that taking more of the surrounding sequence context into account improved model performance across all features. This is likely due to the inherent 
structural complexities of DNA. Contrary to popular belief, human promoters are not characterized by short, easily identifiable regions like a TATA box, which is only present 
in about 15–17% of promoters. Instead, they lack strict sequence conservation and are usually defined by broader, more complex regional features (Putta & Mitra, 2010).Even 
so, the model excelled at picking up these regional characteristics, identifying promoters with the greatest accuracy. 

While intron regions rely on highly stereotyped, universal sequence boundaries to dictate splicing, these splice sites constitute only a tiny fraction of the total sequence 
length. The rest of the intron and exon bodies are highly variable. In contrast, the broader regulatory motif combinations and regional biases of promoters may provide a 
more distinct, continuous signature. This likely explains why the model, relying on sequence context across k values, captured promoter characteristics more effectively than 
the localized boundaries of introns and exons.

























